      "@id": "sdlc/skills/sdlc-core/swarm-monitor.md#provenance-1",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "3. Human-consumable two-table status reporting contract (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "Formalized in cat-harness#1396 (2026-11-23): coordinator swarm updates must stand alone for a human reader outside the worker transcripts, banning naked worker codes, bare PR ranges, and unglossed work-item IDs, and requiring self-contained queue scoreboards and substantive progress rollups.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "cat-harness#1396",
        "2026-11-23"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-11-23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "sdlc/skills/sdlc-core/swarm-monitor.md#provenance-2",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "issue",
      "cat-harness:section": "4. Worker health, memory/swap saturation, idle-on-compute backlog, and Andon-cord triage",
      "prov:value": "Production lesson recorded in cat-harness#1396 (2026-11-23): on macOS and swap-backed Linux, ps -o rss hides runaway memory leaks once pages are compressed or swapped out (for example into /System/Volumes/VM/swapfile*), silently exhausting root disk space (ENOSPC) while resident memory per process remains under 3 GB. Every monitor sweep inspects total memory plus compressed/swap usage alongside df -h / and SIGKILLs runaway subprocesses immediately.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "cat-harness#1396",
        "2026-11-23"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-11-23"
      ]
    },
    {
      "@id": "sdlc/skills/sdlc-core/swarm-monitor.md#provenance-3",
      "@type": "prov:Entity",
      "cat-harness:provenanceKind": "owner-decision",
      "cat-harness:section": "1. Swarm = Parent Work Item & cold-start handover protocol (STRICT)",
      "prov:value": "Extended in cat-harness#1397 (2026-11-24) on three owner directives: (1) every swarm binds to a generic parent Swarm Work Item (epic or milestone in beans, with 'Stability' as one concrete example alongside research, proof-closure, detangling, literature-audit, and outreach swarms) carrying explicit acceptance criteria / end state (EC-1..EC-k), structured child work-item queues (completed, active, queued, idle-backlog), and a Cold-Start Handover Runbook updated and pushed to cat/cat-harness/beans on every routine check-in so any cold successor coordinator or merge manager can take over in under 60 seconds after a stall; (2) workers blocked on single-core compute receive right-scoped non-heavy idle-backlog items in separate worktrees without mid-build git checkout; and (3) the standardized two-table status report is always available on-demand and optionally scheduled periodically per user preference.",
      "cat-harness:refs": [
        "cat-harness#1397",
        "2026-11-24"
      ],
      "dcterms:date": [
        "2026-11-24"
      ]
    }
  ]
